gpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ div.mermaidTooltip{position:absolute;text-align:center;max-width:200px;padding:2px;font-family:-apple-system-body,ui-sans-serif,-apple-system,system-ui,\"Segoe UI\",Helvetica,\"Apple Color Emoji\",Arial,sans-serif,\"Segoe UI Emoji\",\"Segoe UI Symbol\";font-size:12px;background:rgb(48, 48, 48);border:1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.15);border-radius:2px;pointer-events:none;z-index:100;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .flowchartTitleText{text-anchor:middle;font-size:18px;fill:rgb(237, 237, 237);}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ rect.text{fill:none;stroke-width:0;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .icon-shape,#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .image-shape{background-color:rgb(0, 0, 0);text-align:center;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .icon-shape p,#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .image-shape p{background-color:rgb(0, 0, 0);padding:2px;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .icon-shape .label rect,#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .image-shape .label rect{opacity:0.5;background-color:rgb(0, 0, 0);fill:rgb(0, 0, 0);}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .label-icon{display:inline-block;height:1em;overflow:visible;vertical-align:-0.125em;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node .label-icon path{fill:currentColor;stroke:revert;stroke-width:revert;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node .neo-node{stroke:rgb(31, 78, 148);}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].node rect,#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].cluster rect,#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].node polygon{stroke:url(#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_-gradient);filter:drop-shadow( 1px 2px 2px rgba(185,185,185,1));}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].swimlane.cluster rect{filter:none;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].node path{stroke:url(#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_-gradient);stroke-width:1px;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].node .outer-path{filter:drop-shadow( 1px 2px 2px rgba(185,185,185,1));}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].node .neo-line path{stroke:rgb(31, 78, 148);filter:none;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].node circle{stroke:url(#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_-gradient);filter:drop-shadow( 1px 2px 2px rgba(185,185,185,1));}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].node circle .state-start{fill:#000000;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].icon-shape .icon{fill:url(#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_-gradient);filter:drop-shadow( 1px 2px 2px rgba(185,185,185,1));}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ [data-look=\"neo\"].icon-shape .icon-neo path{stroke:url(#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_-gradient);filter:drop-shadow( 1px 2px 2px rgba(185,185,185,1));}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node text{font-size:14px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:normal;fill:rgb(153, 206, 255);}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .edgeLabels text{font-size:13px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.08px;fill:rgb(153, 206, 255);}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node tspan[font-weight=\"normal\"],#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .edgeLabels tspan[font-weight=\"normal\"]{font-weight:600;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .edgeLabel .label rect{opacity:1;rx:13px;ry:13px;fill:rgb(0, 14, 26);stroke:rgb(26, 62, 95);stroke-width:1px;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node rect,#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node circle,#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node ellipse,#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node polygon,#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node path{fill:rgb(0, 40, 77);stroke:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);stroke-width:1px;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node rect{rx:16px;ry:16px;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .node.mermaid-decision .label-container{fill:rgb(0, 14, 26);stroke:rgb(26, 62, 95);stroke-dasharray:2,2;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .edgePaths .flowchart-link{stroke:rgb(175, 175, 175);stroke-width:1px;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ .marker{fill:rgb(175, 175, 175);stroke:rgb(175, 175, 175);}#chatgpt-mermaid-_r_dci_ :root{--mermaid-font-family:-apple-system-body,ui-sans-serif,-apple-system,system-ui,\"Segoe UI\",Helvetica,\"Apple Color Emoji\",Arial,sans-serif,\"Segoe UI Emoji\",\"Segoe UI Symbol\";}DeveloperVVA CLI - Node.jsGit CollectorCommit Metadata, FileVersions and DiffsStructured Project Data JSONPython AI EngineNVIDIA APIStructured AI Response JSONPython Document EnginePDF / DOCX / PPTOutput Directory\n\n\n\n\n\nThe data exchange between modules should have clearly defined formats. This will allow each teammate to develop and test their module independently before the components are integrated.\n8. VVA command-line interface\nVVA is designed as a CLI tool so that developers can use it from their existing development environment without needing a separate graphical application.\nThe main commands have distinct responsibilities.\nvva init\nInitializes VVA for a project, checks the Git repository, and creates VVA configuration, logging and output directories.\nvva status\nDisplays repository information, current branch, Git status, latest commit and whether VVA has been initialized.\nvva report\nCollects Git data, invokes the AI and document modules, and ultimately produces a technical report through the integrated workflow.\nvva watch\nIntended to monitor development activity and trigger collection and documentation when relevant changes or commits are detected.\n\nThe key difference is that vva report is intended to execute the full pipeline, while vva status provides information and vva init prepares the environment.\nvva watch is a later automation feature. Monitoring Git activity, triggering the AI and generating documents automatically should be treated as separate implementation milestones, not assumed to be complete just because the command exists.\n9. Example of a complete VVA workflow\nSuppose a developer is working on an online ticket-management application.\nThey add ticket filtering and commit the change:\ngit add .\ngit commit -m \"Add ticket filtering\"\n\n\nThe intended VVA workflow is:\nStep 1 — Collect the development activity\nVVA identifies the repository and commit, then retrieves the changed files, commit metadata, relevant committed file versions and diff.\nStep 2 — Build the project-data JSON\nVVA stores the collected information in a structured format, such as:\n{\n  \"repository\": \"MiniDesk\",\n  \"branch\": \"main\",\n  \"analyzedCommit\": \"abc123\",\n  \"changedFiles\": [\n    {\n      \"status\": \"M\",\n      \"file\": \"src/tickets/ticket.controller.js\",\n      \"content\": \"[Committed file content]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"diff\": \"[Commit-specific patch]\"\n}\n\n\nThis is an illustrative example; the real paths and values depend on the repository.\nStep 3 — Analyze the work\nThe Python AI engine receives the project data and identifies the implementation details supported by the code changes.\nStep 4 — Prepare the document data\nThe AI returns a structured explanation that can be reused by different document templates.\nStep 5 — Generate the output\nThe document engine uses that information to create a report, synopsis or presentation, and VVA saves the result to its output directory.\nStep 6 — Report the result\nThe CLI tells the developer which files were generated and where they were saved.\nThis end-to-end workflow is the main demonstration VVA should ultimately support.\n10. Team responsibilities\nThe project is divided among three team members, with each responsible for a separate layer of the system.\nPerson 1 — Abhiram Binil\nNode.js · Git · Orchestration\nAbhiram develops the CLI and the central workflow controller. His work includes repository detection, Git operations, commit-specific data collection, file-system management, JSON handling, Python process communication, integration, and error handling.\n\n\nPerson 2 — Vishnu G Pillai\nPython · AI · NVIDIA API\nVishnu develops the AI engine that receives project data, analyzes commit messages and code changes, and returns structured technical explanations for the document-generation module.\n\n\nPerson 3 — Vinay Bisht\nPython · Document Generation\nVinay develops the PDF, DOCX and PowerPoint generation modules, including templates, formatting and the conversion of structured AI output into final documents.\n\n\nAlthough the work is divided, all three members need to understand the complete workflow and be able to explain their contributions during the project presentation and viva.\n11. Technology stack\nThe planned technologies are organized by responsibility.\nLayer\tTechnology\tPurpose\nCLI and orchestration\tNode.js, JavaScript ES modules\tCommands, process coordination and workflow control\nVersion control\tGit\tCommit history, diffs, file versions and repository metadata\nData exchange\tJSON\tTransfer project data and AI results between modules\nAI engine\tPython, NVIDIA API\tAnalyze changes and generate structured explanations\nDocument generation\tPython\tCreate and format documents\nPDF output\tReportLab\tGenerate PDF reports\nDOCX output\tpython-docx\tCreate editable Word documents\nPPT output\tpython-pptx\tGenerate PowerPoint presentations\nThe libraries listed are planned implementation choices. They should be considered part of the completed stack only when they have actually been integrated and tested.\n12. Configuration, files and data management\nVVA needs to manage both its own configuration and the data it generates.\nAn intended project layout could look like this:\nvva/\n├── package.json\n├── README.md\n├── .gitignore\n├── src/\n│   ├── commands/\n│   │   ├── init.js\n│   │   ├── status.js\n│   │   ├── report.js\n│   │   └── watch.js\n│   └── core/\n│       ├── git.js\n│       ├── collector.js\n│       └── python.js\n├── ai/\n│   └── ai_engine.py\n├── document-engine/\n│   └── document_generator.py\n├── docs/\n├── examples/\n└── .vva/\n    ├── config.json\n    ├── logs/\n    └── output/\n\n\nThis is a proposed structure and may differ from the actual repository as development progresses.\nThe .vva directory stores VVA-specific configuration, logs and generated output. The repository's Git metadata and the developer's source code remain managed by Git and the existing project.\nConfiguration should eventually support settings such as the default document format, output directory and Python environment. Sensitive API keys should be kept out of source control.\n13. Important engineering considerations\nFor VVA to generate useful documentation, collecting data successfully is not enough. The workflow must also be reliable.\nAccuracy: Analyze the exact commit being documented and avoid mixing changes from unrelated commits.\nError handling: Handle repositories without commits, missing parent commits, deleted files, missing Python installations and failed AI requests.\nData size: Large diffs or entire source files may exceed AI model limits. VVA should select relevant context, limit payload size where necessary and report when information has been omitted.\nSecurity and privacy: Source code can contain credentials or confidential information. VVA should avoid sending secrets to an external AI service, protect API keys, and provide appropriate control over which files and diffs are processed.\nOutput validation: The AI's response should be checked against an expected schema before document generation begins.\nTraceability: Each generated report should identify the repository and commit it describes, so the developer can trace the documentation back to the underlying development work.\nThese considerations are part of making VVA a dependable engineering tool rather than a demonstration that works only for one sample commit.\n14. Current development status\nBased on the work discussed so far, VVA has an initial implementation of its Node.js foundation.\nImplemented or tested in earlier steps\n- CLI initialization and status commands.\n- Git repository detection and basic Git operations.\n- Collection of commit metadata and changed files.\n- Collection of Git diffs.\n- Saving project data to .vva/output/project-data.json.\n- Initial Node.js-to-Python process communication.\n- Retrieving the previous file version for deleted files.\n\n\n\nRemaining work to verify or complete\n- Accurate collection of changes for a selected commit, including edge cases.\n- Robust validation and error handling.\n- Integration with Vishnu's NVIDIA-powered AI engine.\n- Integration with Vinay's document-generation module.\n- A complete vva report workflow that generates a real document.\n- Automatic monitoring through vva watch.\n- End-to-end testing, documentation and demonstration preparation.\n\n\n\nThe exact status of the latest commit-specific collector changes and the other teammates' modules still needs to be verified. A successful Git collection test does not by itself mean the full AI-to-document pipeline is finished.\n15. Future scope\nOnce the core workflow is reliable, VVA could be extended with additional capabilities.\n- Pull request documentation: Generate summaries from pull-request changes and merge history.\n- Release notes: Summarize changes across a range of commits or a release.\n- Scheduled progress reports: Generate periodic development summaries.\n- Custom document templates: Allow teams to select report formats and section layouts.\n- Multiple projects: Manage documentation workflows for multiple repositories.\n- Configurable automation: Let users decide when VVA should run and which outputs it should generate.\n- Improved change analysis: Group related commits and identify larger features across several files.\n- Additional integrations: Connect to issue trackers or project-management systems if the project later requires them.\nThese are potential extensions, not features that need to be completed for the first working version.\n16. Expected outcome\nThe expected outcome is a functional command-line application that connects Git, AI-assisted analysis and automated document generation.\nA successful implementation should demonstrate that:\n1. A developer can initialize VVA in a Git project.\n2. VVA can collect accurate information about a real commit and its code changes.\n3. The collected data can be passed to an AI engine.\n4. The AI engine can return structured, usable analysis.\n5. The document engine can transform that analysis into a formatted document.\n6. VVA can coordinate the process and report the location of the generated output.\nThe project can be useful in academic software projects, development teams, progress reporting, technical documentation and project presentation preparation.\n17. The core value of VVA\nVVA's main contribution is automating the journey from software development activity to technical documentation.\nWithout VVA, developers may need to review Git history, interpret changes, write explanations and format documents as separate tasks. VVA aims to coordinate those activities through a single workflow.\nIts defining idea is:\nGit records the work → AI explains the work → the document engine presents the work → VVA coordinates everything.\nThat is the complete vision of VVA — Version & Workflow Automato"
